From f0c56c451af6d95e389c081401ae4fba46e6e8d9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 18:52:54 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] media: mediatek: mt6797-vdec: support capture buffers created
 at any time

VA-API clients through libva-v4l2-request create CAPTURE buffers one at a
time with VIDIOC_CREATE_BUFS, also after STREAMON, and their pools can
hold more than 17 buffers. The driver only worked for buffers that
existed at STREAMON and had vb2 indices below 17:

- start_streaming() allocated a collocated-MV buffer for each capture
  buffer existing at that moment. Later buffers had none, so H.264 and
  HEVC rejected them.
- H.264 and HEVC programmed the vb2 index as the hardware picture slot
  and rejected indices of 17 and above.

Allocate the MV buffer in buf_init() and free it in buf_cleanup(), sized
for the codec and CAPTURE format at that time; VP9 still gets none. The
OUTPUT format can still change while CAPTURE buffers exist, so the
decoders also reject a buffer whose MV buffer is too small for the
current format. STREAMON zeroes the MV buffers, as the fresh allocations
did before.

Map the current picture and its DPB references to hardware slots 0..16
per context. A buffer keeps its slot while it is the current picture or
in the DPB and loses it when it leaves. A buffer without a slot takes its
own index when that is free, otherwise the lowest free slot. Sixteen
references and the current picture always fit; anything more is
rejected. With up to 17 buffers every buffer keeps slot = index, so the
programming is unchanged for such pools in any order of use.

The HEVC error-concealment state (which buffers hold a picture and its
POC) describes capture buffers rather than hardware slots, so key it by
vb2 index over all VIDEO_MAX_FRAME buffers.
